The most important part of the template is the docstring being at the top,
which is there to tell the user what the function does.
The structure also includes that you must not use the abbreviated names
of the standard imports in the docstrings.
Having the parameters are very useful, from experience,
it basically tells the user what type of inputs it takes at
which position. Not only does it tell you which type of inputs
the function takes, it also explains in detail what they are specifically.
Returns are important because it tells the user what items are
outputted and how it can be manipulated.
Other Parameters are there to tell the user the keywords that are
used less and or the parameters that commonly used.
Raises is important because it tells the user what went wrong, why,
and where.
See Also is important because it tells can inform the user of other
functions that do the same job in a different way or in some way
related to the current function.
Notes can include information about how the algorithm was implemented,
being as long as it needs to be. Math can also be found here.
References is used to cite relevant literatures that can be used when
explaining where certain ideas come from.
Examples is very important because it gives a quick rundown of how
the function will act in an easy to follow scenario. Furthermore,
it can give more information about where to find more examples.
Revisions is important because it tells the user when the latest updates
to the function were created.
